.pdxdoc-version 1
.tool           libpdx-argv
.kind           library
.version        1.0.0
.author         paideia-os-team
.wave           R49
.summary        CLI argument parsing (long primary, short one-per-hyphen)
                for every R49+R50 P0 tool; also decodes the alternate
                semantic-pipe invocation record.

.section NAME
libpdx-argv - argv parser for PaideiaOS tools

.section SYNOPSIS
    #include this library in a tool's link line via `deps.list`
    then, at _start:

        FlagSpec::reset()
        StdVocab::register_all()
        FlagSpec::register(<tool-flag-name>, <kind>, <id>)
        ...
        ParsedArgs::reset()
        let err = Parser::parse_argv(argv, argc)

    or, when invoked through a semantic pipe:

        let err = SchemaInvoke::parse_from_schema_record(rec_ptr, rec_len)

.section DESCRIPTION
libpdx-argv turns a `_start`-time argv (or an equivalent
`PdxArgvRecord@0.1` semantic-pipe record) into a `ParsedArgs`
struct that a tool's dispatch loop reads by id. The library ships
no default caps and issues no syscalls of its own; every consumer
declares its own caps in its own `caps.decl`.

Two invocation paths converge on the same `ParsedArgs`:

  argv (textual)  --parse_argv-->  ParsedArgs
  wire (typed)    --parse_from_schema_record-->  ParsedArgs

The dispatch code that follows is invocation-path-agnostic; a
tool that renders `--help` via `doc <tool>` (see `HelpBackend`)
does so identically whether the user typed the flag or a peer
tool set the flag through a pipe.

.section FLAG GRAMMAR
The R49 grammar is fixed (see D3 in `design/tooling/plan.md`):

  --foo             boolean long flag
  --foo bar         long flag with lookahead value
  --foo=bar         long flag with inline value
  -x                single-letter short flag (one per hyphen)
  --                sentinel; all remaining tokens are positional
  -                 bare hyphen; positional (stdin convention)

.section BREAKS WITH POSIX
Clustered short flags (`-la`, `-abc`) are REJECTED with error
code `ERR_CLUSTERED_SHORT`. Every short flag must have its own
hyphen (`-l -a`). This is deliberate: the semantic pipe binds
flags by id, and clustering collides with typed short flags that
consume an argument (e.g. `-n 5` in `head`). See D3 for the full
rationale.

Long flags may accept a `=` separator OR a lookahead token, at
the tool's choice per `FlagSpec::register` kind. The `:` separator
is reserved for `--no-cap:<name>` (I3-vocabulary) and for future
namespaced-flag forms; consumer tools may not repurpose it.

.section DUPLICATE FLAGS
Repeating a flag is never an error: every occurrence gets its own
storage slot and `flag_count` grows with each one. Three accessors
read the result (`libpdx-argv.ENH-008`):

  find_flag_by_id(id)       FIRST occurrence (default; unchanged
                             since M2)
  find_last_flag_by_id(id)  LAST occurrence — a later flag
                             overrides an earlier one, e.g.
                             `--color=auto --color=never` resolves
                             to `never`. This is the mainstream-CLI
                             convention and what makes shell
                             aliases composable (an alias that
                             appends `--color=never` wins over
                             whatever the user typed earlier).
  count_flag_by_id(id)      occurrence count, for flags where
                             repetition itself is the signal
                             (`-v -v -v` raising verbosity by 3).

A tool picks whichever accessor matches its own flag's semantics;
libpdx-argv does not force one policy on every flag.

.section STANDARD FLAGS (I3 vocabulary)
`StdVocab::register_all` binds these nine ids into `FlagSpec`
for every tool that calls it. IDs 1..9 are RESERVED for these
flags; a tool's own flags start at id 100.

  id 1  --help           back-end forwards to `doc <tool>`
  id 2  --version        emits `<tool> <semver>` on stdout
  id 3  --dry-run        report what would change; don't do it
  id 4  --json           emit output as line-delimited JSON
  id 5  --schema         list this tool's declared output schemas
  id 6  --verbose        raise output verbosity by one step
  id 7  --quiet          suppress non-error output
  id 8  --color=<mode>   `auto`, `always`, `never`
  id 9  --no-cap:<name>  strip the named cap before exec (I7)

.section TYPED FLAG ARGUMENTS
`Typed::parse_int_u64` / `parse_size` / `parse_timespan` decode
the string half of a typed flag into a `(ok: u64, value: u64)`
pair, returned directly in `rax:rdx`. These are leaf functions —
they never touch `ParsedArgs::error_code` at all; no
`ParsedArgs` constant exists for a typed-decode failure. A failing
decode (`ok == 0`) still leaves `flag_values[k]` pointing at the
offending text (the
parser stored that pointer regardless of what the value turns
out to decode to), so the tool renders its own diagnostic from
`ok` plus that pointer — this library does not do it for you.

  --older-than 7d       parse_timespan -> 604800 (seconds)
  --older-than 3h       parse_timespan -> 10800
  --size 2m             parse_size     -> 2097152
  --size 3g             parse_size     -> 3221225472
  -n 42                 parse_int_u64  -> 42

The `w` (week) suffix on `parse_timespan` is deferred to M6 (a
per-tool concern once anything ships a `--older-than 1w` need).

.section ALTERNATE INVOCATION (SEMANTIC PIPE)
When a tool is invoked through a `KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT` semantic
pipe with a `PdxArgvRecord@0.1` payload rather than through the
loader-seeded argv slot, the launcher hands the tool the
record's `(ptr, len)` in place of `(argv, argc)`. The tool
routes to `SchemaInvoke::parse_from_schema_record` instead of
`Parser::parse_argv`. Everything downstream — id-keyed dispatch,
typed-value decoding, `--help` forwarding — is identical.

The wire form is documented in `design/architecture.md` §10.1
and its schema fingerprint is registered with
`libpdx-semantic-pipe` M2 envelope framing.

Every offset the record carries (`name_off`, `value_off` when
present, `pos_off`) is bounds-checked against the record length
before it is dereferenced, and the resulting string is scanned
for a NUL terminator before the record ends — see DIAGNOSTICS
below. This is the library's only untrusted-input surface (a
peer process across the pipe), and no lookup is performed on a
pointer that fails either check.

.section --HELP BACK-END
`HelpBackend::fill_doc_argv` synthesises a two-slot argv of the
shape `["doc", tool_name]` in caller-provided storage and hands
it to the consumer, which then reaches `doc` through the shell.
libpdx-argv itself does not spawn `doc`; the consumer's own
loader-supplied cap chain decides whether the doc invocation is
permitted.

.section --SCHEMA
`SchemaEmit::register(schema_name_ptr)` at init lets a tool
publish which schemas it emits on stdout under normal operation.
`--schema` is ordinary `StdVocab` id 5 (see STANDARD FLAGS above)
— `Parser::parse_argv` stores it like any other registered flag;
it does not intercept it specially. The consumer's dispatch loop
checks `ParsedArgs::find_flag_by_id(StdVocab::STD_ID_SCHEMA)`,
and on a hit walks `SchemaEmit::get_count` / `SchemaEmit::get_name`
to print the declared schemas one per line, then exits 0. (This is
a different flag from the well-known `--pdx-schema` inline compare
described under ALTERNATE INVOCATION's `emit_schema` bit — `--schema`
lists declared output schemas; `--pdx-schema` is an M1 debug toggle
unrelated to SchemaEmit.)

Consumers that emit no schema-bearing output register nothing
(the flag is still accepted; the tool exits 0 with empty stdout).

.section CAPABILITY REQUIREMENTS
libpdx-argv itself requires none (see `caps.decl`). It reads
only the caller's own argv memory, and for the `--foo=bar` form
mutates that memory in place to null-terminate on `=`. It runs
synchronously inside the caller's process and issues no
syscalls.

.section DIAGNOSTICS
`Parser::parse_argv` and `SchemaInvoke::parse_from_schema_record`
both set `ParsedArgs::error_code`; `ERR_OK` (0) is success, and every
non-zero value is one of the constants below (all defined in
`src/parsed_args.pdx`; this list and that file's `pub let ERR_*` set
match exactly — libpdx-argv.ENH-005). Every non-zero code also sets
`ParsedArgs::error_arg_index` to the offending argv slot (argv path)
or flag/positional loop index (schema-record path); `0` for a
whole-buffer header failure.

  ERR_FLAG_OVERFLOW (1)             more than 32 flags in one parse
  ERR_POS_OVERFLOW (2)              more than 32 positionals in one parse
  ERR_CLUSTERED_SHORT (4)           `-la` etc. (D3 rejects clustering)
  ERR_LONG_MISSING_NAME (5)         `--=foo`, `--:foo`
  ERR_MISSING_VALUE (6)             typed flag reached end of argv with
                                     no value to consume
  ERR_SCHEMA_BAD_MAGIC (7)          schema-record: bad wire magic
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED_VERSION (8) schema-record: version != 1
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_BAD_LAYOUT (9)         schema-record: header too short, a
                                     count exceeds MAX_FLAGS/MAX_POS, or
                                     the body-fits check failed
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_BAD_OFFSET (10)        schema-record: name/value/pos
                                     offset out of range
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_UNTERMINATED (11)      schema-record: string ran to record
                                     end with no NUL
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG (12)             unrecognised long/short name
                                     (opt-in: FlagSpec::set_strict(1);
                                     libpdx-argv.ENH-004. Permissive by
                                     default — an unregistered flag is
                                     silently accepted as boolean
                                     unless strict mode is enabled.)

`ERR_UNKNOWN_ARG_FORM` (3) is reserved in `parsed_args.pdx` but no
code path returns it yet. Typed-decode failures (`Typed::parse_*`
returning `ok == 0`) do not set `error_code` at all — see TYPED FLAG
ARGUMENTS above — and the schema-record path's only failure codes
are the four listed above; no other constant exists for it.

This library does not itself call `exit` or prescribe a process exit
status for any code above — mapping `ParsedArgs::error_code` to a
concrete exit number (and rendering the human-readable diagnostic on
stderr) is entirely the consumer's own I4 exit-code-discipline
obligation (`design/tooling/plan.md` T-INV-I4). Do not treat any
exit-status split by "code group" as a contract this library defines.

